WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:03.040
With one spin-off underway&nbsp;
and another in the queue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.960
"Yellowstone" is showing no signs of slowing&nbsp;
down when it comes to keeping viewers coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:10.960
back — or tuning in for the first time&nbsp;
— week after week. Without a doubt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:15.040
a major component of the series' appeal&nbsp;
is its most rugged anti-hero, Rip Wheeler,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:19.280
whose devotion to John Dutton and the&nbsp;
Yellowstone Ranch appears to know no bounds.

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:23.520
And yet, there are bounds when it comes to what&nbsp;
audiences are willing to accept with regard to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:28.000
the often criminal, inevitably violent actions&nbsp;
Rip feels he must take in order to defend the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.400
Duttons and their ranch. Throughout Season&nbsp;
4, another beloved character, Lloyd Pierce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:36.880
has butted heads with one of the other ranch&nbsp;
hands, Walker. Walker "stole" Lloyd's girlfriend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:40.720
and Lloyd has been unable to keep his&nbsp;
jealousy and anger at bay ever since.

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:44.240
After Lloyd breaks the "no fighting&nbsp;
in the bunkhouse" rule in Episode 4,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:47.120
Rip and John ultimately decide the&nbsp;
two should work their issues out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:51.200
by pounding the crap out of one another,&nbsp;
though John has one more wrinkle to add.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.600
"I want you to make an example&nbsp;
out of the last man standing."

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:01.200
Rip not only pummels Lloyd repeatedly in Episode&nbsp;
4 after the latter's initial fight with Walker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:06.160
but he also beats him down aggressively in Episode&nbsp;
6, then graphically smashes Lloyd's hand with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:10.863
boot. Lloyd's second beating, importantly,&nbsp;
isn't Rip's way of reiterating this rule.

00:01:10.863 --> 00:01:14.480
"There's no fighting on this ranch.&nbsp;
if you want to fight someone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:16.800
you fight me. I'll fight you all f–king day."

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:21.920
When Rip destroys his best friend's hand, it's&nbsp;
because Lloyd refuses to back down from a fight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:26.000
that John Dutton ordered Rip to have Lloyd and&nbsp;
Walker undertake until they could "work out" their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:30.000
issues. By the time Rip "nobly" shoulders&nbsp;
the burden of taking Lloyd down for good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.600
Lloyd's been continually fist fighting&nbsp;
with Walker for well over an hour.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:37.280
Prior to the premiere of Season 4, fans&nbsp;
were already speculating about Rip and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:40.720
Lloyd's fight from the trailers and had&nbsp;
some pretty distinct opinions about what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:44.720
they felt it would take to justify such&nbsp;
a thing. As one fan wrote on the series'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:48.960
subreddit, Lloyd would have to "commit&nbsp;
a cardinal sin for Rip to beat him up."

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:53.360
Lloyd's "cardinal sin," on the surface, was&nbsp;
stabbing Walker. Scratch below that surface,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:57.520
however, and it becomes clear that his real sin&nbsp;
was putting his own personal grudge against Walker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:03.120
over the safety of the ranch — or so we're told.&nbsp;
However, it's a bit more complicated than that.

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:07.600
As viewers will recall, Kayce was supposed to&nbsp;
kill Walker in Season 2, after the singer saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:12.320
Rip help Jamie cover up a murder. Kayce, however,&nbsp;
couldn't bring himself to kill Walker, a misstep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:17.360
Rip and Lloyd discover in Season 3, Episode 9. But&nbsp;
Walker doesn't actually return to The Yellowstone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:22.160
Ranch on his own. Walker shows up in Dillon. Had&nbsp;
Lloyd and Rip simply walked away from the bar and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:26.000
pretended they never saw Walker, they wouldn't&nbsp;
be obligated to keep him under the thumb of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:30.480
ranch at all, since he clearly wasn't interested&nbsp;
in reporting anything he saw to the authorities.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:33.440
Thus, the better part of the&nbsp;
situation that leads to Rip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:37.200
"being forced" to beat down on Lloyd is&nbsp;
a situation largely of his own making,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:42.080
and subsequently difficult to chalk up to just&nbsp;
another instance of "cowboy justice." Add to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:46.160
that the fact that Walker and Lloyd's entire&nbsp;
conflict does little, if anything, to further&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:50.880
the plot of the series and you have the makings of&nbsp;
a scene that simply crosses one bridge too many.

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:54.160
When it comes to anti-heroes like&nbsp;
Rip, audiences can accept otherwise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:57.440
unacceptable levels of violence since&nbsp;
they're tethered to the narrative.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:00.800
These acts would be appalling to our sense&nbsp;
of right and wrong in the real world, but,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:05.040
within the fictitious universe, they can be&nbsp;
justifiable. It's only when that violence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:09.040
spills outside the bounds of that justification&nbsp;
— either because we've gained too much sympathy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:12.720
for the individual on the receiving end, or,&nbsp;
because it doesn't come off as a legitimately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:17.120
necessary action — that our willful suspension&nbsp;
of real-world morality begins to break down.

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:20.320
Viewers not only have enormous&nbsp;
amounts of sympathy for Lloyd,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:23.760
but it's also difficult to understand the&nbsp;
thinking that went into throwing two ranch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:27.600
hands and integral employees into a&nbsp;
Gladiator-style fight to the finish,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:32.480
only to then punish one of those individuals for&nbsp;
doing exactly what he was told. It's so difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:36.560
to understand, in fact, that several viewers on&nbsp;
one Reddit thread theorized that the scene only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:40.960
really existed because "the show is setting up a&nbsp;
situation for Rip to take Lloyd for granted, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:46.000
then realize he was wrong." Whether that theory&nbsp;
pans out or not, we'll just have to wait and see.

